/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The glide slope is usually 3° and is placed so that the path crosses the runway threshold about 50 ft (15 m) up, the threshold crossing height (TCH).
   - The rate of descent is ground speed × tan 3°.
   - The ILS reports an angle off the path, not metres.
   - Decision height (DH) is the height above the threshold at which the pilot must see the runway environment or go around; for CAT II and III it is read on the radio altimeter.
   - Below CAT II limits the pilot cannot see enough to land by hand, so the system lands.
   - Two words describe how failures are handled.
*/
